# HQL collections

HQL distinguishes membership, order and lookup through three collection types.
These are language-design contracts, not implemented collection APIs.

**This card owns the account of order.** Positional order, value order and their
agreement are set out under *Ordered is not orderable* below, and the cards that
touch order — [[orderable]], [[hql/core]], [[type-system]] — link here rather
than restating it.

| Type | Intended meaning | Design consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Set<T>` | Unordered collection of distinct elements | No implicit first element or stable iteration order |
| `Seq<T>` | Positionally ordered sequence, allowing repeated elements | Positions are observable; the *elements* need not be orderable |
| `Map<K, V>` | Values associated with distinct keys | Lookup is by key; iteration order is not implied |

Equality, Card identity, key eligibility, missing-key results and duplicate-key
construction policy need explicit definitions before implementation. Set
membership must not accidentally equate distinct claims merely because they
have the same endpoints or text.

## Cards are a set

```hql
cards : Set<Card>
```

This is a type statement for the context value, not a proposed standalone
declaration syntax. `cards` represents the current vault or namespace. Sorting
can establish a sequence; merely discovering files must not establish a language
ordering guarantee. Selecting a reproducible prefix requires a defined order,
including how equal sort keys are handled.

`cards` is a `Set<Card>`, and `Seq<T>` is what a `sort` or a ranking produces
when order matters. A prefix of a set is reproducible because its elements carry
their own ordering key — see [[orderable]].

## There is no `List`

`List<T> <: Seq<T>` was declared and is withdrawn. `Seq<T>` is an abstract type
governed by a law — a position exists for every element — and `List<T>` was a
claim about **representation**: that the elements are held rather than produced,
so a length is known and a second traversal costs nothing. A claim about
representation earns a name only when the language needs to tell two
representations of one abstraction apart.

Nothing does. No step produces a sequence lazily, the evaluator's `Value::Seq`
holds its elements outright, and the checker resolved `List` to `Seq` from the
day it was written — so the binary never distinguished them either. A name whose
only content is a distinction nothing observes is a name a reader has to date.
`Value::Seq` keeps its name honestly, because there is one representation to
have.

If HQL later finds the case — a streamed ranking, a bounded result set, a
sequence produced on demand — the name comes back with the case that justifies
it, which is the order these things should arrive in.

## Ordered is not orderable

Three words in this area are close enough to be confused, and they are three
different things. This is settled, and the register matters as much as the
vocabulary.

| Concept | What it says | Whose property |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Seq<T>` | **Positional order.** A position exists for every element: `0 → 42`, `1 → 7`, `2 → 19`. | the collection |
| `Orderable` | **Value order.** For any two values of the type, which comes first is determinable — `∀ a, b : T`, `a ≤ b` can be decided. | the element type |
| *agreement* | **Both, and they coincide.** The positional order is the value order. | the collection, relative to its elements |

The load-bearing sentence:

> **A sequence is ordered, but its elements need not be orderable.**

`Seq<Function>` is the case that proves it. It has a first, a second and a third
element, and no two functions have a meaningful `<` between them. The positions
come from the sequence, not from the values — which is the exact mirror of
`cards : Set<Card>`, where the order comes from the values and not from the
collection.

So the phrase "`Seq` is orderable" is wrong and should not appear. A `Seq` is
**ordered**, or **indexed**. `Orderable` is reserved for a type whose values
possess an ordering relation. Sorting expects that relation to be **total**, or
the result is not determined.

The third row names a **relation between the first two**, not a third type.
`SortedSeq<T>` was named in this card and declared nowhere, and it stays
undeclared for the reason `List` does: minting a type for it would only be worth
doing if something downstream needed to know that a sequence is sorted, and
nothing does. `take` over a `Seq` keeps the order it finds without asking where
that order came from.

The language does have the case, and it already has a name for it.
`Ranking<T>` (see [[hql/semantic-search]]) is a `Seq<Hit<T>>` whose positions coincide with
its hits' value order, because `Hit <: Orderable` orders by score. A general
`SortedSeq<T>` would be a second name for the only shape that has it.

### Sorting is an operation, not a type property

"Sortable" names something a function can do with a collection, not a
foundational property a type has. It is therefore a signature and not a
supertype:

```hql
fn sort<T>(xs: Seq<T>) : Seq<T>
where T <: Orderable
```

or, when the caller supplies the key rather than the element type carrying one:

```hql
fn sort<T, K>(xs: Seq<T>, by: T -> K) : Seq<T>
where K <: Orderable
```

The second form is what makes `Seq<Function>` sortable without `Function` being
`Orderable` — the order arrives with the key. It takes a **key extractor** and
not a comparator, which is what `cards | sort(by = c => c.title)` already does,
so no `Ordering` type is needed and none is declared. A comparator would be a
second way to say the same thing, needing a type whose only use is to be its
result.

This is also why [[orderable|`Orderable`]] stays a supertype rather than becoming
a capability: it says the values *have* a key, and supplying one from outside is
the other signature, not the same one narrowed.

## Collection functions

Filtering, mapping, sorting and aggregation should be ordinary typed functions
used through [[pipes]]. Their signatures must make collection behavior clear.
Filtering a set can preserve membership semantics. Mapping a set raises a choice:
whether equal outputs collapse into a set or another collection retains them.
That choice, generic function syntax and conversion names remain open.

Unordered inputs give an executor room to partition pure work. Sequence order,
duplicate behavior and aggregation laws constrain that freedom. A parallel
execution strategy must preserve the result promised by the collection function.

See [[hql/core]] for the type vocabulary and [[hql/knowledge]] for semantic identity.
